    "math_academy_id": "q-61906",
    "knowledge_point_id": "947eb2bb-0443-5740-9d45-847c70877270",
    "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Surface Area of a Polyhedron Given Some Lengths",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003941/assets/586b2c9d3409b68436f16715937b7a4bf0abdf8c1a05c628ccafdc49cf6d641d.png)\n\nWhat is the total surface area of the cube above if it is $4\\,\\text{cm}$ long, $4\\,\\text{cm}$ wide, and $4\\,\\text{cm}$ tall?",
    "worked_solution": "First, let's consider the net of our plank.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14003941/assets/2c8a118f8f3c3ccba67e25ae09096ef1ec0cf15fc6a5e7f9de68f797b7105c80.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have squares only.\n\nThe squares are $4\\,\\text{cm}$ long and $4\\,\\text{cm}$ high. So, the area of one square is:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area of the square}=4\\times 4=16\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the surface area of the cube consists of $6$ squares. \n\nTherefore, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Surface area} & =6\\times (\\text{Area of the square}) \\\\  & =6\\times 16 \\\\  & =96\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
